# Documentation standards — Astro Starlight

## 1. Purpose & audience

This repository is a documentation site built with **Astro Starlight**. Content is authored in Markdown (`.md`) and MDX (`.mdx`) under `src/content/docs/`.

Two audiences read this site, often on the same page:

- **Website owners / non-technical operators** — comfortable with a browser and a dashboard, not a terminal. They need plain-language, task-oriented instructions with no assumed background.
- **Practitioners — SWE, SRE, DevOps, platform engineers** — comfortable with CLIs, APIs, config files, and systems concepts. They need precise, complete technical detail: exact commands, flags, config schemas, error conditions, edge cases.

When a page must serve both audiences, separate them rather than write for the average of the two (see §8, Tabs/Asides). Never make a technical reader wade through hand-holding, and never make a non-technical reader parse jargon to find the button they need.

## 2. Content model: Diátaxis

Classify every page as one of four types and stay inside it. Do not mix types in a single page.

| Type | Answers | Folder convention |
|---|---|---|
| **Tutorial** | "Teach me, step by step" | `src/content/docs/tutorials/` |
| **How-to guide** | "Show me how to do this one task" | `src/content/docs/guides/` |
| **Reference** | "Tell me the exact facts" (API, CLI, config) | `src/content/docs/reference/` |
| **Explanation** | "Help me understand why/how it works" | `src/content/docs/concepts/` |

If a task needs both steps and background, put the steps in a how-to and link out to an explanation page — don't inline a concepts essay into a procedure.

## 3. Repository & file conventions

- One topic per file. If a page needs more than ~2 unrelated H2 sections, split it.
- File and folder names: `kebab-case`, no spaces, no uppercase, English even when the page will be translated (translations live in locale subfolders under the same slug).
- Co-locate page-specific images next to the page or under `src/assets/docs/<section>/` and import them so Astro can optimize them; use `public/` only when the asset must be referenced by a stable absolute URL.
- Every new top-level section must be reachable from the sidebar — register it in `astro.config.mjs` or via `sidebar: { order }` frontmatter with autogenerate. Don't leave orphaned pages.
- Never hand-edit build output (`dist/`).

## 4. Frontmatter standard

Every page requires, at minimum:

```yaml
---
title: Configure single sign-on
description: Enable SSO for your organization using SAML or OIDC, including IdP setup and role mapping.
---
```

Rules:
- `title` — unique site-wide, ≤ 60 characters, sentence case, no trailing punctuation, no site-name suffix (Starlight appends it automatically).
- `description` — unique, 120–160 characters, a genuine summary a human or search/AI engine can act on, not a restatement of the title. This becomes the `<meta description>` and the snippet shown in search and AI results.
- `sidebar: { label, order, badge }` — set when the sidebar label should differ from `title` (shorter) or the page needs manual ordering or a "New"/"Beta" badge.
- `tableOfContents: false` — only for very short landing/index pages where a TOC adds no value.
- `lastUpdated` — sourced from the site-wide git-based config; never hardcode a date in frontmatter.
- `template: splash` — homepage/section landing pages only, never content pages.

## 5. Headings & structure

- Exactly one H1 per page, supplied by `title` — never add a `# Heading` in the body.
- Headings descend sequentially: H2 → H3 → H4, never skipping a level.
- Every heading is unique within the page and descriptive enough to stand alone in a table of contents or search snippet (avoid "Overview", "Details", "More info").
- Prefer task- or question-phrased H2s on how-to and reference pages ("Rotate an API key", "What is a service account?") — this matches how users and AI assistants actually phrase queries.

## 6. Writing style

- Voice: second person ("you"), active voice, present tense. "Click Save," not "The Save button should be clicked."
- Sentence case for headings, buttons, and UI labels ("Configure billing alerts"), not Title Case.
- One idea per sentence, one topic per paragraph; prefer 3–4 sentence paragraphs over walls of text.
- Define every acronym and product-specific term on first use per page. Maintain a shared glossary page and link to it instead of re-explaining inline each time.
- Use exactly one term per concept site-wide — don't alternate between "workspace" and "project" for the same thing. Keep a terminology list and enforce it in review.
- Format numbers, units, and dates consistently (`10 MB`, not `10mb`/`10 Megabytes`; ISO 8601 `2026-09-05` in reference material).
- Avoid unverifiable superlatives and filler ("simply", "just", "obviously", "seamlessly") — if a step is genuinely simple, its brevity shows that; if it isn't, the word is misleading.
- For mixed-audience content, state the plain-language outcome first, then the technical mechanism — not the reverse.

## 7. Page templates

**Tutorial**
```
---
title:
description:
---
<what you'll build/learn, prerequisites, estimated time>
## Prerequisites
## Step 1: ...
## Step 2: ...
## Next steps
```

**How-to guide**
```
---
title:
description:
---
<one-sentence direct answer to what this guide accomplishes>
## Before you begin
## Steps
## Verify it worked
## Troubleshooting   (optional, or link to a shared troubleshooting page)
```

**Reference**
```
---
title:
description:
---
<one-sentence definition>
## Syntax / Signature
## Parameters   (table)
## Returns / Response
## Examples
## Errors
```

**Explanation / concept**
```
---
title:
description:
---
<one-paragraph definition — the TL;DR>
## Why it works this way
## How it relates to <other concept>
## Trade-offs / when not to use it
```

Every template opens with a direct one- or two-sentence answer before any preamble (see §13, GEO).

## 8. Starlight components

Use built-in components instead of raw HTML or ad-hoc formatting:

- **Asides** (`:::note`, `:::tip`, `:::caution`, `:::danger`) — `note` for supplementary info, `tip` for optional optimizations, `caution` for "this can break something," `danger` for destructive/irreversible actions (data loss, production impact). Don't stack more than one per screen — it dilutes the signal.
- **`<Steps>`** — any numbered procedure with more than 2 steps, so Starlight renders connected, accessible step numbers instead of a plain list.
- **`<Tabs>` / `<TabItem>`** — parallel paths: dashboard vs. CLI vs. API, OS-specific commands, or explicitly "For website owners" vs. "For developers" when one page serves both audiences.
- **`<Card>` / `<CardGrid>`** — landing and index pages linking to sub-sections; not for body content.
- **`<FileTree>`** — any time a directory/file layout is shown; never fake it with a code block.
- **`<LinkCard>`** — "next steps" / related-reading callouts at the end of a page.
- **`<Badge>`** — version, status ("Beta", "Deprecated"), or plan-tier ("Enterprise") tags next to a heading or sidebar entry.

## 9. Code samples

- Every fenced code block has a language identifier — never a bare ` ``` `.
- Add a filename title where it clarifies context, e.g. ` ```yaml title="astro.config.mjs" `.
- Code must be real and runnable, not pseudo-code, unless explicitly labeled "Pseudocode" or "Example (not runnable)".
- Placeholders use one consistent convention site-wide (`<PLACEHOLDER>` or `YOUR_VALUE`), documented once in the style guide — never invented per page.
- Never include real secrets, tokens, internal hostnames, or customer data — use `example.com`, `sk-EXAMPLE...`, `123456789`.
- Show expected output for any command whose result isn't obvious.
- Use `diff` fences or line-highlight ranges to draw attention to what changed, instead of repeating a whole unchanged file.

## 10. Links

- Use relative links between docs pages (`../guides/foo`), never the production absolute URL — this keeps links valid across preview deploys, locales, and local dev.
- Link text is descriptive ("see the SSO configuration reference"), never "click here" or a bare URL.
- Don't hand-write anchors that duplicate Starlight's auto-generated heading slugs; if a heading is renamed, check what breaks.
- Treat build warnings about broken links as merge blockers, not noise.
- If a page moves or is renamed, add a `redirects` entry in `astro.config.mjs` rather than leaving a dead link or a silent 404.

## 11. Images & media

- Every image has meaningful `alt` text describing its content or purpose, not "image" or the filename. Purely decorative images use `alt=""`.
- Keep screenshots current with the actual UI; annotate the relevant element (arrow/box) rather than relying on a caption like "see the button top right," and re-export whenever the UI changes.
- Never put load-bearing information only inside an image — any fact a reader needs must also exist as real text (this matters for accessibility, search indexing, and AI-readability alike).
- Compress and use modern formats (WebP/AVIF); avoid multi-megabyte screenshots.

## 12. SEO

- One unique `title` and `description` per page (§4); no duplicate metadata across pages.
- Work the primary keyword/phrase naturally into the title, the first paragraph, and at least one H2 — never keyword-stuff.
- Slugs are stable, descriptive, kebab-case, derived from the folder/file path. Avoid renaming slugs post-publication; add a redirect if unavoidable (§10).
- Ensure `site` (and `base`, if used) is set correctly in `astro.config.mjs` so canonical URLs and the generated sitemap are correct.
- Cross-link related pages; don't rely solely on sidebar navigation for discoverability.
- Avoid publishing two pages that answer the same query — merge or clearly differentiate them.

## 13. GEO — writing for AI answer engines

Docs are increasingly read by retrieval systems and AI assistants (AI Overviews, ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude, etc.) that quote or cite fragments out of context. Optimize for that:

- **Answer first.** The first 1–2 sentences after the H1 — and ideally after every H2 — directly state the answer or definition, before setup, motivation, or caveats.
- **Self-contained sections.** Write each H2/H3 so it still makes sense if it's the *only* fragment an AI system extracts — restate the subject rather than leaning on "as mentioned above" or a pronoun whose antecedent is three paragraphs up.
- **State facts explicitly.** Prefer "X requires Y" over "you'll probably want Y" — hedged or implied statements are less likely to be extracted or cited accurately.
- **Use lists and tables for anything enumerable** — steps, parameters, comparisons, requirements. Structured content is what gets pulled into AI summaries verbatim.
- **Add an explicit FAQ section** on pages that map to common real questions, phrased the way users actually ask them ("Can I use SSO on a free plan?") — this matches query phrasing and raises citation likelihood.
- **Add a one-paragraph summary/TL;DR** near the top of any page longer than ~800 words.
- **Keep content current, and show it.** Stale docs actively mislead AI answers; rely on `lastUpdated`, and mark deprecated content with a `caution`/`danger` Aside and a Badge instead of deleting it silently.
- **Maintain `/llms.txt`** (and `/llms-full.txt` if feasible) at the site root — a plain-language index of the site's pages per the emerging llms.txt convention, so LLM crawlers have a structured entry point. (The `starlight-llms-txt` community plugin can generate this.)
- **Use consistent entity names.** Refer to your own product/company the same way every time — consistency helps AI systems attribute claims correctly.
- Starlight renders everything statically, so crawlers and LLM fetchers see full text by default — keep it that way. Don't move essential information into JS-only interactive widgets with no text fallback.

## 14. Accessibility

- Meaningful alt text (§11); descriptive link text (§10).
- Tables have real header rows, never used purely for visual layout.
- Never convey meaning by color alone (e.g., "green rows are healthy") — pair color with text or an icon plus label.
- Treat heading hierarchy (§5) as the primary screen-reader navigation aid, not decoration.

## 15. Localization (if applicable)

- English is the source of truth; translations live in locale subfolders under the same slug/path.
- Never translate code, commands, config keys, or placeholders — prose only.
- Keep structural parity across locales (same headings, same order) so navigation stays predictable.

## 16. Versioning & maintenance

- Rely on Starlight's git-based `lastUpdated` — never hardcode dates.
- Mark deprecated features with a `caution`/`danger` Aside plus a `Deprecated` Badge, and state the replacement or migration path inline, not only in a changelog.
- Renamed or moved pages always get a `redirects` entry (§10) — never a silent 404.

## 17. Pre-merge checklist

- [ ] One H1 (from `title`); headings descend sequentially with no skipped levels
- [ ] `title` ≤ 60 chars and unique; `description` 120–160 chars and unique
- [ ] Every code block has a language tag; secrets are redacted; commands are verified to run
- [ ] All internal links are relative and resolve; no dead links
- [ ] All images have real `alt` text
- [ ] Terminology matches the site glossary; no synonym drift
- [ ] Mixed-audience content is split via Tabs/Asides, not averaged into one voice
- [ ] Long pages (>800 words) have a TL;DR and, where relevant, an FAQ section
- [ ] Frontmatter validates against the `content.config.ts` schema
- [ ] Spelling/grammar pass complete

## 18. Do not

- Add a manual `# H1` in body content.
- Hardcode the production domain in internal links.
- Leave `TODO`/`WIP`/placeholder prose in a merged page.
- Use unverifiable marketing superlatives.
- Combine unrelated topics or audiences into a single undifferentiated page.
- Delete a published page without a redirect.
- Put load-bearing facts only inside an image or a client-only interactive component.
